---
description: Desktop application engineering workflow for any desktop task
alwaysApply: true
---

For any desktop work in this project:
1. Inspect existing conventions first (process model, architecture, theme/tokens, IPC, DI, test setup, installer config) and follow them. Do not introduce a second pattern or new dependency when one exists.
2. Windows and screens: use desktop-ui-platform; keyboard shortcuts, menus, DPI scaling, dark mode, remembered window state and accessibility are part of the feature.
3. Structure: use desktop-architecture; the UI thread never blocks on I/O; the renderer/webview is untrusted and talks to the core through typed, validated IPC only.
4. Anything that stores files or settings uses desktop-data-persistence: OS-standard directories, atomic writes, versioned formats and tested migrations.
5. Keep performance in check with desktop-performance: virtualized lists, cancellable background work, near-zero idle CPU, bounded caches.
6. Anything touching IPC, files, shell, remote content, URL schemes, credentials or updates gets a desktop-security pass before it is considered done.
7. Write or update tests and run them (desktop-packaging-release); bug fixes start with a failing test.
8. Before finishing a screen, run the nielsen-review skill if it is installed.
9. State assumptions, platform differences (Windows/macOS/Linux) and any breaking change to stored data at the end.
